{"file":{"content":"The remaining problem is no longer “spectral gap for a mysterious operator.” It is:\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t\\text{a boundary-scale incomplete }3\\text{-adic character-sum estimate.}\n}\n]\n\nEverything else has been pushed either into exact vanishing or into exactly marginal counting. That is a good place to be, because the last estimate has a very concrete local form.\n\n## 1. Current proof state\n\nThe finite side now looks like this:\n\n[\n\\text{classification}\n\\Rightarrow\n\\text{strict finite-level gap}\n]\n\n[\n\\text{pair-correlation phase formula}\n\\Rightarrow\n\\text{exact nonstationary vanishing}\n]\n\n[\n\\text{walk-window factorization}\n\\Rightarrow\n\\text{collision flattening}\n]\n\n[\n\\text{lift-sum factorization}\n\\Rightarrow\n\\text{independent one-variable coset sums}\n]\n\nand the only open finite estimate is:\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\td=m-1\\text{ transition-zone coset sums must save a fixed factor on chains.}\n}\n]\n\nThe repeated marginality is important. It says any argument that says “bound the remaining phases by (1)” cannot win. The gap is exactly where the phases still oscillate at the boundary scale.\n\n## 2. Resolve the (d=m-1) boundary first\n\nThis should be the immediate target. You need a complete classification of one-variable coset sums\n\n[\nS(R;\\alpha,m,c)\n===============\n\n\\sum_{T\\bmod 3^m}\n\\chi\\bigl(R(\\alpha+3^{c-m}T)\\bigr)\n]\n\nin the boundary case\n\n[\nd=v_3((\\log R)'(\\alpha))=m-1.\n]\n\nYour current vanishing lemma handles\n\n[\nd\\le m-2.\n]\n\nFor (d=m-1), the first-order phase lives exactly at cube-root scale. So after Postnikov expansion it should reduce to something of the form\n\n[\nS\n=\n\n\\chi(R(\\alpha))\n\\sum_{T\\in\\mathcal D}\n\\exp\\left(\\frac{2\\pi i}{3}\\lambda T\\right)\n]\n\nup to a harmless constant, where\n\n[\n\\lambda\\in(\\mathbb Z/3\\mathbb Z)^\\times\n]\n\nunless a genuine degeneracy occurs, and (\\mathcal D) is the actual lift-domain modulo (3).\n\nThis explains the observed behavior:\n\n[\n\\mathcal D=\\mathbb Z/3\\mathbb Z\n\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad\nS=0;\n]\n\n[\n|\\mathcal D|=2\n\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad\n|S|/|\\mathcal D|=\\frac12;\n]\n\n[\n|\\mathcal D|=1\n\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad\n|S|/|\\mathcal D|=1.\n]\n\nSo the boundary bookkeeping probably reduces to a **domain-completeness classification**, not a valuation issue.\n\nThat matches your diagnostic: the nominal derivative valuation is correct in most cases; the non-vanishing is not because the derivative vanished, but because the lift-domain is incomplete at the final mod-(3) layer.\n\n## 3. State the boundary lemma you need\n\nI would aim for this exact lemma.\n\n[\n\\textbf{Boundary coset lemma.}\n]\n\nLet (\\chi) be primitive modulo (3^c), and let (R) be one of the lift-ratio functions arising in the chain trace. Suppose\n\n[\nv_3((\\log R)'(\\alpha))=m-1.\n]\n\nThen the normalized coset sum satisfies\n\n[\n\\left|\n\\frac{1}{|\\mathcal D|}\n\\sum_{T\\in\\mathcal D}\n\\chi(R(\\alpha+3^{c-m}T))\n\\right|\n\\in {0,\\tfrac12,1}+O(3^{-1})\n]\n\nmore precisely determined by the image of the admissible lift-domain (\\mathcal D) modulo (3). The value (1) occurs only when the endpoint constraints force a single residue class modulo (3).\n\nThen the finite-side gap reduces to showing that the (|S|=1) cases are either thin, nilpotent in chains, or coboundary-classified.\n\nThat is the right boundary target.\n\n## 4. What to compute next\n\nDo not compute spectra first. Compute the boundary coset types.\n\nFor every transition-zone configuration with\n\n[\nd=m-1,\n]\n\nrecord the following tuple:\n\n[\n(\\lambda,\\ \\mathcal D\\bmod3,\\ |S|/|\\mathcal D|,\\ \\text{word type},\\ \\text{endpoint type},\\ \\text{weight}).\n]\n\nHere\n\n[\n\\lambda\n=======\n\n3^{-(c-1)}\n\\left[\n\\log R(\\alpha+3^{c-m})-\\log R(\\alpha)\n\\right]\n\\pmod3.\n]\n\nThe audit should produce a table:\n\n[\n\\begin{array}{c|c|c|c}\n\t\\mathcal D\\bmod3 & \\lambda & |S|/|\\mathcal D| & \\text{mass}\\\n\t\\hline\n\t{0,1,2} & \\lambda\\ne0 & 0 & \\cdots\\\n\t\\text{two residues} & \\lambda\\ne0 & 1/2 & \\cdots\\\n\t\\text{one residue} & \\lambda\\ne0 & 1 & \\cdots\\\n\t\\lambda=0 & \\text{degenerate} & 1 & \\cdots\n\\end{array}\n]\n\nThe most important number is not the average value. It is the **transition operator spectral radius** after replacing each boundary link by its normalized absolute coset sum:\n\n[\n0,\\quad \\frac12,\\quad 1.\n]\n\nIf the boundary transition operator has spectral radius (<1) after removing coboundaries, the finite H23a theorem closes.\n\n## 5. Build the boundary transition operator\n\nDefine a finite directed graph whose vertices are the chain states surviving prefix-locking. For each boundary link (e), assign weight\n\n[\n\\beta(e)\n========\n\n\\left|\n\\frac{1}{|\\mathcal D_e|}\n\\sum_{T\\in\\mathcal D_e}\n\\chi(R_e(\\alpha+3^{c-m}T))\n\\right|.\n]\n\nSo\n\n[\n\\beta(e)\\in{0,\\tfrac12,1}\n]\n\nin the ideal boundary classification.\n\nThen study the weighted transition matrix\n\n[\n\\mathcal B\n]\n\nwith entries\n\n[\n\\mathcal B_{uv}\n===============\n\n\\sum_{e:u\\to v}\np(e)\\beta(e).\n]\n\nThe desired theorem is:\n\n[\n\\rho(\\mathcal B)<1\n]\n\nuniformly in the primitive non-coboundary sector.\n\nThis is the clean replacement for all the earlier marginal counting. Counting corresponds to (\\beta(e)=1) everywhere and gives exactly zero margin. The real theorem is that the actual (\\beta(e)) matrix has spectral radius (<1).\n\nThis is probably the right final finite-side object.\n\n## 6. What to prove after the boundary audit\n\nOnce the audit shows the pattern, the proof should split into three lemmas.\n\n### Lemma A: Postnikov boundary reduction\n\nProve that every (d=m-1) lift sum reduces to a mod-(3) additive character sum:\n\n[\nS\n=\n\n\\chi(R(\\alpha))\n\\sum_{T\\in\\mathcal D\\bmod3}\ne_3(\\lambda T),\n\\qquad \\lambda\\ne0,\n]\n\nunless the link belongs to an explicitly classified degenerate/coboundary family.\n\nThis is classical (p)-adic stationary phase at the boundary scale.\n\n### Lemma B: Domain classification\n\nClassify the possible lift-domain reductions (\\mathcal D\\bmod3). Ideally:\n\n[\n\\mathcal D=\\mathbb F_3\n\\quad\\text{or}\\quad\n|\\mathcal D|=2\n]\n\nfor all non-coboundary transition links, while (|\\mathcal D|=1) links cannot form recurrent chains except inside the coboundary sector.\n\nIf true, the worst recurrent boundary factor is (1/2), giving a fixed gap.\n\n### Lemma C: Boundary transition contraction\n\nShow that the weighted boundary transition matrix satisfies\n\n[\n\\rho(\\mathcal B)\\le 1-\\delta.\n]\n\nThis is the final finite estimate.\n\nThis route avoids needing a global square-root bound. You only need fixed contraction in the transition layer.\n\n## 7. Be careful with the (|S|=1) cases\n\nThe value (1) is dangerous. It may still be harmless, but only if one of the following is true:\n\n1. (|S|=1) links are **thin** in total weight;\n2. they are **acyclic/nilpotent** in the boundary chain graph;\n3. they force the exact coboundary relation already classified;\n4. they are followed within bounded time by a (0) or (1/2) link.\n\nThis is now the precise rigidity question.\n\nThe strongest possible statement would be:\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t\\text{A recurrent chain made only of }|S|=1\\text{ boundary links is exactly a coboundary chain.}\n}\n]\n\nSince coboundaries are already classified, this would close the transition-zone estimate.\n\nThis is the finite analogue of the global principle:\n\n[\n\\text{no phase cancellation}\n\\Rightarrow\n\\text{coboundary}.\n]\n\n## 8. How the Postnikov formula should look\n\nFor primitive (\\chi) modulo (3^c), write on principal units\n\n[\n\\chi(1+3z)\n==========\n\n\\exp\\left(\\frac{2\\pi i A z}{3^{c-1}}\\right)\n]\n\nup to the usual higher-log correction, equivalently\n\n[\n\\chi(u)=e_{3^c}\\bigl(A\\log u\\bigr),\n\\qquad 3\\nmid A.\n]\n\nFor\n\n[\nu(T)=R(\\alpha+3^{c-m}T),\n]\n\nexpand\n\n[\n\\log u(T)\n=========\n\n\\log u(0)\n+\n3^{c-m}T(\\log R)'(\\alpha)\n+\n\\frac12 3^{2(c-m)}T^2(\\log R)''(\\alpha)\n+\\cdots.\n]\n\nIf\n\n[\nv_3((\\log R)'(\\alpha))=m-1,\n]\n\nthen the linear term has valuation\n\n[\n(c-m)+(m-1)=c-1.\n]\n\nThus the primitive character sees exactly\n\n[\ne_3(\\lambda T).\n]\n\nThe quadratic term has valuation\n\n[\n2(c-m)+v_3((\\log R)'')\n]\n\nand should be (\\ge c) in the clean boundary case. If sometimes it is (c-1), that explains boundary irregularity. So one diagnostic should compute:\n\n[\nv_3\\left(3^{2(c-m)}(\\log R)''(\\alpha)\\right).\n]\n\nIf it is always (\\ge c), the boundary is purely linear. If not, you need a quadratic Gauss-sum boundary classification.\n\nThis is an important check.\n\n## 9. Immediate numerical checklist\n\nRun this exact checklist for the (d=m-1) configurations:\n\n1. Compute\n\n[\n\\lambda_1=\n3^{-(c-1)}\n3^{c-m}(\\log R)'(\\alpha)\n\\pmod3.\n]\n\n2. Compute\n\n[\n\\lambda_2=\n3^{-(c-1)}\n3^{2(c-m)}(\\log R)''(\\alpha)\n\\pmod3.\n]\n\n3. Record whether (\\lambda_2=0).\n\n4. Record the lift-domain mask\n\n[\n\\mathcal D\\bmod3\\subseteq\\mathbb F_3.\n]\n\n5. Predict the sum using\n\n[\n\\sum_{T\\in\\mathcal D}e_3(\\lambda_1T+\\lambda_2T^2).\n]\n\n6. Compare to the exact coset sum.\n\nIf this prediction matches all boundary cases, you have resolved the (d=m-1) bookkeeping.\n\n## 10. Manuscript update\n\nYour paper should now have a final “finite-side endpoint” section with this structure:\n\n[\n\\textbf{Proved:}\n]\n\n[\n\\text{exact vanishing for }d\\le m-2;\n]\n\n[\n\\text{collision flattening;}\n]\n\n[\n\\text{lift-sum factorization;}\n]\n\n[\n\\text{marginality of all counting-only estimates.}\n]\n\n[\n\\textbf{Remaining:}\n]\n\n[\n\\text{transition-zone boundary sums }d=m-1.\n]\n\nThen state:\n\n[\n\\textbf{Transition-zone estimate.}\n]\n\nThere exists (\\delta>0) such that the boundary transition operator built from the normalized incomplete coset sums has spectral radius (\\le1-\\delta) outside the coboundary sector.\n\nThis is the final finite-side conjecture.\n\n## Bottom line\n\nThe next targets are now very concrete:\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t\\text{1. Classify the }d=m-1\\text{ coset sums by Postnikov expansion.}\n}\n]\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t\\text{2. Build the boundary transition matrix with weights }0,\\tfrac12,1\\text{ or quadratic variants.}\n}\n]\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t\\text{3. Prove recurrent }|S|=1\\text{ chains are coboundary/thin/nilpotent.}\n}\n]\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t\\text{4. Deduce a fixed contraction of the boundary transition operator.}\n}\n]\n\nThat is the cleanest path to finishing the finite H23a theorem.\n","filePath":"/home/john/mynotes/deterministic_equidistribution_theory/critiques/dc_17.md","numLines":508,"startLine":1,"totalLines":508},"type":"text"}